# SDOC — Implementation Status @sdoc-impl-status
{
    # Meta @meta
    {
        type: doc

        status: active

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    # About @about
    {
        Implementation status for the SDOC project. Tracks what is completed, what is in progress, and what is next. Read this first when starting a new task.
    }

    # Current State @current-state
    {
        SDOC v0.2.24 is the current release. The parser, HTML renderer, slide renderer, slide export pipeline (PDF, PowerPoint / Google Slides, Claude Slides) and VS Code extension are stable and published to both npm and the VS Code Marketplace (auto-updates enabled). The format supports scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table formulas, email auto-detection, collapsible scopes in all HTML output, and a full inline formatting suite. Issues are open on github/markup and github-linguist for GitHub rendering and language recognition.

        The spec is expected to evolve. The core principle of explicit brace scoping is stable. Everything else is open to change, with the constraint that existing documents must remain valid or be mechanically migrateable ([R10](requirements.sdoc#r10)).
    }

    # Completed @completed
    {
        The parser, renderer, and tooling are feature-complete for v0.2. See `specification.sdoc` for the full feature set. Key milestones:

        {[.]
            - v0.2.0 — scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table width/alignment, key:value meta, line comments, K&R brace style, braceless scopes, implicit root scope
            - v0.2.4 — escaped pipes in table cells
            - v0.2.5 — auto-detected email addresses, file watcher for external preview writes
            - v0.2.6 — table formulas (=SUM, =AVG, =COUNT, arithmetic, A1 references, circular reference detection)
            - v0.2.7 — VS Code Marketplace publishing (entropicwarrior-msenfin.vscode-sdoc), progressive disclosure API documented
            - v0.2.8 — collapsible scopes and copy-to-clipboard in all standalone HTML output
            - v0.2.12 — citations (\`[@key]\` inline references, \`{[citations]\` definition blocks, auto-numbering by order of first appearance, back-links, validation)
            - v0.2.13 — column directives: per-column alignment (\`< > =\`) and number formatting (\`$\`, \`,.N\`, \`.N\`, \`%\`) in table directive rows
            - v0.2.19 — structured slide layouts, theme loading with \`theme.json\`, geometry harvest, PowerPoint / Google Slides export
            - v0.2.21 — optional slides, held back from the formats a deck is sent on in
            - v0.2.22 — drilldown navigation driven by position rather than a build-time class; independent navigation axes (left/right is the spine, up/down the column); deck images resolved against the \`.sdoc\` and embedded in every output format
            - v0.2.24 — the default design box became 1920x1080, the canvas a Claude Slides artifact is fixed at, with the print page following at 20 x 11.25in (the same geometry at 96 dpi). Nothing changes on screen, since a deck is scaled to the window whatever its box; the Claude Slides export now copies lengths across instead of scaling them. The theme's type scale was deliberately left in proportion rather than raised to that format's recommendation, which overflowed two example slides
            - v0.2.23 — the \`scatter\` layout; slide background images with placement, scale, flip and linear or radial fades; a Claude Slides artifact export with its own validator and a pull/diff that reads a collaborator's edits back; a deck-scoped stylesheet through the existing \`style-append:\` key; the presenter filmstrip; and two silent-corruption fixes — a scope left unclosed no longer parses to a different tree without saying so, and a theme's \`text-transform\` no longer turns "40 mW" into "40 MW"
        }

        # GitHub Adoption
        {
            - [x] Issue opened on github/markup for SDOC rendering support (github/markup#2030)
            - [x] Issue opened on github-linguist for .sdoc language recognition (github-linguist/linguist#7838)
            - [x] sdoc2html CLI translator built as contribution artifact for github/markup
            - [x] llms.txt at repo root for AI agent discovery
        }
    }

    # In Progress @in-progress
    {
        - [ ] Awaiting feedback on github/markup issue (#2030) and github-linguist issue (#7838)
        - [ ] Growing .sdoc file count on GitHub toward linguist recognition threshold (~200 unique repos)

        # Slide Layouts and Export @slides-wip
        {
            Unreleased, on \`feat/slide-layouts\`. Structured slide layouts (\`columns\`,
            \`stats\`, \`pipeline\`, \`matrix\`, \`rows\`, \`bars\`, \`split\`, \`stack\`),
            theme loading with \`theme.json\` and CSS asset inlining, a geometry harvest
            that measures a built deck in headless Chrome, and PowerPoint / Google Slides
            export driven by that measurement. See
            [slide-authoring.sdoc](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc).

            - [x] PDF print layout: the print stylesheet forced \`.slide\` to \`display: block\`, so a theme's \`justify-content\` went inert and a \`margin-top: auto\` collapsed. Every slide centred on screen printed hard against the top of the page. \`.slide\` is a flex column in print now, and \`.slide-content-scale\` inherits its flex rules rather than naming layouts
            - [x] **Silent content loss in every PDF, predating the above.** \`fitSlidesForPrint\` measured content against the slide's box, which overstates the room by the theme's padding on all four sides, so an overflowing slide was clipped by that much and never scaled at all. Making the wrapper a flex box then hid half the remaining overflow, because a centred box spills past both ends and \`scrollHeight\` sees only what is below the top edge. It now measures the wrapper's own box, unstretched and start-aligned, and leaves a scaled slide start-aligned — \`scale()\` does not relayout, so centred overflow stays clipped however far it shrinks. Measured: a 28-line slide lost its title and 10 lines before the fix, keeps all 28 after
            - [x] \`background-fade\` survives a PDF on macOS. A CSS mask with a transparent stop becomes a graphics-state soft mask of type \`/S /Alpha\`, which Quartz — Preview and Quick Look, what opens when someone double-clicks an emailed deck — draws as a hard edge. \`--pdf\` and \`--pptx\` now composite the picture, its fade and the slide's ground colour into one flat image (\`src/slide-fade-bake.js\`). The HTML build is untouched and needs no browser. Compositing onto an opaque ground emits a JPEG and the PDF comes out *smaller* than the masked original (505KB to 353KB on a full-bleed photo); a ground that is not an opaque flat colour falls back to alpha, which is correct but several times larger, and says so. This also gives \`--pptx\` the fade, which it dropped entirely before
            - [x] \`--artifact\` could not export any deck containing an image. The exporter writes an \`sdoc-asset:<file>\` placeholder, because an image cannot carry its \`/_blob/<id>\` until the publish step has uploaded the file and been told the id — and the subset validator rejected that placeholder as an invalid \`<img>\` src, so the export reported an error per image and wrote nothing. The two halves of the documented flow disagreed. The validator now accepts the placeholder only when the caller opts in, which the exporter does and nothing else does, so a file reaching a publish with one unresolved still fails. \`tools/artifact-resolve-assets.js\` already exits non-zero on a leftover, so the publish-time guarantee was never the gap
            - [x] A conformance suite for the Claude Slides export (\`test/test-artifact-conformance.js\`). It reads the feature list out of \`src/slide-layouts.js\`, so a layout or config key with no deck exercising it fails the suite instead of shipping untested; a deliberate gap goes in \`KNOWN_GAPS\` with a reason, and a gap that later gets covered fails until its entry is removed. The export of both example decks is checked in under \`test/artifact-golden/\`, so the subset validation and the fidelity guards run with no browser and Chrome is needed only to prove the goldens still match. Each guard was verified to fire against a deliberately broken slide, not just to pass
            - [ ] The harvest does not read \`box-shadow\` or \`text-shadow\` at all, so a theme's shadows are dropped from a Slides export with no warning — unlike a pseudo-element, which is at least reported. The subset carries both properties
            - [ ] The subset validator accepts **any** value for \`box-shadow\`, \`text-shadow\` and \`filter\` without checking grammar or ranges (\`slide-artifact-validate.js\`, the \`return null\` by "ranges are not re-checked here"). A malformed shadow publishes, and a slide carrying one renders as a blank page rather than losing a property. The conformance suite guards the shapes we emit; the validator should check the grammar itself — colour last, blur within 64 for text and 160 for box
            - [ ] The exporter raises text under 24px to that floor, but \`a peer repo\` measured a published deck with 127 elements down to 8.67px all rendering at their authored size, and the type's \`format.md\` calls the floor "not build-checked". If it is advisory, \`--artifact-keep-small-text\` is the wrong default and the export is changing decks for no reason
            - [x] A conformance suite for the PPTX export (\`test/test-pptx-conformance.js\`), the sibling of the artifact one. A \`.pptx\` is a zip of XML, so the golden is a structural digest — per slide, what the harvest found against what reached the file — which is reviewable in a diff where raw OOXML is not. Guards: every slide reaches the file, no text is lost between the harvest and the runs, every image atom becomes a picture, a deck with images ships media. Each verified to fire against a planted digest
            - [x] **SVG shapes reached the PPTX.** Each \`<svg>\` is rasterised to a PNG in a pass before the measuring walk and arrives as one image atom, fonts baked in at the theme's own face — which also settles the SVG-\`<text>\`-in-a-fallback-face problem for this format. Two causes, not one: the walk had no case for \`<path>\`/\`<circle>\`/\`<rect>\`/\`<line>\`, *and* \`isTextLeaf\` accepted an \`<svg>\` as an inline child, so a box holding a labelled diagram was classified as a text leaf and the drawing was never visited at all. The rasterised branch sits after the painted-box check, because the frame can be on the \`<svg>\` element itself. The artifact export was never affected: it carries SVG markup through opaquely
                        - [x] The artifact exporter built one style string per node and put it on whatever tag it emitted, so a picture carried the type it inherited and the subset refused it — 26 of 35 errors on a real deck were \`font-size is not allowed on <img>\` and its siblings. Declarations are now filtered per emitted tag through \`PROPS_FOR_TAG\`, exported from the validator so there is one table rather than two opinions
            - [x] A theme written with \`color-mix(in srgb, …)\` was refused outright: Chrome serialises it as \`color(srgb r g b / a)\` and the subset has no \`color()\` function. Normalised to \`rgba()\` in \`colourOf\`, which every colour already passes through. Same bug class as a colour-first shadow — a computed-value serialisation that is valid CSS and outside the subset's grammar
            - [x] Speaker notes were refused for being over the 4,000-character limit when they were not. The exporter truncates to exactly 4,000 and *then* escapes, and \`textOf\` in the validator returned text as written, so an \`&\` counted as five characters. The five slides of a real deck that failed at 4,020-4,140 were all inside the limit as a reader counts it. \`textOf\` now decodes entities, so the limit measures content rather than escaping; genuinely over-long notes are still truncated with a warning, which the exporter already did
            - [ ] The exporter raises text under 24px to that floor. \`a peer repo\` has now confirmed from \`format.md\` that the floor is explicitly **"not build-checked"**, and measured 169 elements down to 8.67px rendering at their authored size in a published artifact. So the raise changes a deck's design for a rule the platform does not enforce, and \`--artifact-keep-small-text\` is probably the wrong default. Flipping it changes every deck's output, so it wants a decision rather than a commit
            - [x] Every heading arrived bold. The format is explicit that \`font-size\` and \`font-weight\` never flow into \`<h1>\`-\`<h3>\`, and the subset's headings default to 600 — while the exporter emitted a value only when it differed from the inherited one, the usual harmless economy. So a 400-weight heading under a 400-weight parent emitted no weight at all and rendered at 600, on every heading of every deck. 62 headings in this repo's own examples were wrong. Invisible to a validator, because the output is perfectly admissible; it reads as "the type looks a bit off"
            - [x] A hairline border rounded away to nothing. A theme's \`0.666667px\` frame is antialiased into a real line by a browser and dropped by a renderer that rounds — 26 borders across 8 slides of one deck. Any border that exists is now emitted as at least 1px, because the alternative is not a thinner line, it is no line
            - [x] Text was pinned at its box rather than at its ink. A \`Range\` over the contents reports where the glyphs are, which a table cell's vertical centring or a negative \`text-indent\` moves. Two guards, the second because the first introduced a defect: vertically, follow the ink only when the Range is *shorter* than the box, since a \`line-height\` below the face's natural one makes the inline box overhang and the Range's top then lies; horizontally, only when the ink escapes the content box to the left, and touching x and w only
            - [x] A pseudo-element the theme paints is exported as a pinned box instead of being warned about and dropped. It has no node for a walk to find, so its box is reconstructed from the computed style — an absolutely positioned one from \`left\`/\`top\` against its containing block, an in-flow one at the start of the host's content box — and its own transform is then applied, because a \`translate(-50%,-50%)\` is what puts a disk *on* a line rather than beside it. Emitted before the body so it sits behind the content. Dropping one is worse than a missing mark: a pseudo-element occupies space, so losing it also displaces whatever shared its box
            - [x] Three things about a picture the subset cannot say are painted rather than described. \`object-fit\` is only \`cover\` or \`contain\` there, and the exporter was not even reading the computed value — it *guessed* from the aspect ratios, so a picture the theme asked to \`fill\` was cropped and zoomed. A \`filter: drop-shadow\` follows the alpha channel while a \`box-shadow\` follows the rectangle, so translating one to the other draws a hard box round a cut-out. And \`transform\` allows only \`scale(N)\` with N in 0.5-2, so a mirror cannot be expressed at all. A picture needing any of those is baked into a canvas at its exact box, which *is* \`fill\`, mirrored if the page mirrors it, with each shadow painted alone by pushing the picture clear of the canvas so only its shadow lands — a canvas shadow follows alpha as the filter does. The shadow margin is clamped per side, because the padded picture is its own box and the page clamps a negative offset to 0, which would slide the picture inwards: lose shadow at an edge rather than move the thing casting it. Everything else passes through with the fit CSS actually resolved
            - [ ] Gate \`--check\` consistently: it is ignored with \`--pdf\` and unconditional with \`--pptx\`, and never sets a non-zero exit code
            - [ ] Decide whether the new modules join the package \`exports\` map or leave the npm tarball — they ship today but are unreachable
            - [ ] Warn when a theme's \`slide\` box and \`page\` size disagree at 96 dpi, instead of silently clipping the PDF
            - [x] The harvest dropped text sitting directly inside an element that also has element children — it walked \`children\` rather than \`childNodes\`, so prose beside a figure simply vanished and the only sign was a shorter slide. A bare text run is carried as a text node of its own now, positioned with a \`Range\`
            - [x] An inline mark set larger or in another face was flattened to its parent's type, because the subset has no \`font-size\` or \`font-family\` on a span. It becomes a block of its own **only when it is the whole of its parent**: this export emits flow rather than pinned boxes, so splitting a mark out of the middle of a sentence would stack the words around it as paragraphs. The narrower rule is a real difference from the converter that reported it, whose output is pinned throughout and for which the split is free
            - [ ] \`<br>\` line breaks are still dropped by the harvest
            - [ ] No CI runs the test suites; the geometry and PPTX tests skip themselves when Chrome is absent
            - [ ] The geometry harvest and the fade bake both run in *screen* media, where a deck carries a fit-to-window scale on \`.slide\`. Anything print-only is unguarded by \`--check\`, which is how the print-layout bug above survived a release; and a \`getBoundingClientRect\` in a harvest returns scaled pixels, which is a live trap for the next script written against one
            - [ ] A blurred \`text-shadow\` does not survive a PDF on macOS either (filled grey box behind the text), while \`filter: drop-shadow\` does. Reported by \`a peer repo\` against their own deck's CSS, so it is not an engine bug, but it belongs in the authoring guidance on what survives an export
            - [ ] Chrome's PDF export declares no output intent, so text and vector fills are untagged \`/DeviceRGB\` while images carry ICC profiles. On a wide-gamut display the same colour renders differently in text and in a picture. An sRGB output intent in the export path would fix it for every deck
        }

    }

    # Next Up @next-up
    {
        These are agreed tasks ready to be picked up, not a full backlog.

        # Parser Improvements
        {
            - [ ] A brace-delimited scope left unclosed is auto-closed at EOF with no error, so a missing \`}\` yields a *different tree* and the document still reports as clean. Found in this file: one missing brace nested three sections inside a fourth and moved \`@next-up\` under \`@in-progress\`, and the parser, the test suite and the binding's "every document parses cleanly" check all passed it. Minimal case: \`# A @a { # B @b { x  # C @c { y } }\` parses \`@c\` as a child of \`@b\`. This is the failure [R1](requirements.sdoc#r1) exists to prevent — a scope that opened with \`{\` is not braceless and should be an error at EOF, unlike a genuinely braceless scope
            - [ ] Enforce single root scope (grammar says `document = scope` but parser allows multiple top-level scopes). Parser should warn on violation. See [C1](requirements.sdoc#c1).
            - [ ] Decouple rendering from AST. Current renderer functions are HTML-centric. Need format-neutral AST with separate renderer modules. See [C2](requirements.sdoc#c2).
        }

        # Extension Improvements
        {
            - [ ] Preview should auto-update when switching active editor tabs (currently requires re-running the preview command)
        }

        # Spec Additions
        {
            - [ ] Document metadata ([S2](suggestions.sdoc#s2)) -- key:value meta is implemented; remaining work is defining canonical well-known keys and exposing `meta.properties` to downstream consumers
        }

        # Export Pipeline
        {
            - [ ] SDOC to Google Docs export (for collaboration with non-technical people)
            - [ ] SDOC to DOCX export
            - [ ] Simple Google Docs / DOCX to SDOC import (for round-tripping simple documents)
        }
    }
}
